{"kind":"stack","id":"solar-motion","title":"Planets and the Sun: how they move","theme":{"background":"#0b1d3a","foreground":"#ffffff","font":"Trebuchet MS, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Orbit"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"The curved path a planet travels around the Sun."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Rotation"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"The spinning of a planet on its own axis, which makes day and night."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"image","id":"c3-p","picto":"sun"},{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"What is at the centre of our solar system?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"The Sun","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"The Moon","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Jupiter","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"How long does the Earth take to travel once around the Sun?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"About one year","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"About one day","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"About one month","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-q","content":"What causes day and night on Earth?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"The Earth spinning on its axis","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"The Sun moving across the sky","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-c","label":"The Moon blocking the Sun","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You are an astronaut leaving Earth to visit another planet. Which way do you go?"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-a","label":"Head towards Mars","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Head towards Venus","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"Mars is further from the Sun, so the trip is long and cold. You spot its red dusty surface as you land."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"Venus is closer to the Sun, so it is very hot. It takes Venus about 225 Earth days to orbit the Sun."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Which planet is closest to the Sun?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Mercury","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Neptune","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Saturn","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-h","content":"You finished the solar system motion quiz!"},{"type":"text","id":"c10-m","content":"Great work exploring how planets orbit the Sun and spin on their axes. Keep looking up at the night sky."}],"actions":[]}}
